## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

## 1. Starting endpoint

The exact endpoint is

\[
\zeta=\dim\ker S\ge s,
\qquad s=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int},
\]

equivalently

\[
\operatorname{rank}S\le d_{\rm int}+|C^\partial|-\Delta_\Lambda.
\]

The new results were required to turn the activation flat or a universally locked
first-transition triangle into this source-specific Schur rank saving. They do not.

## 2. Strongest genuinely new audited theorems

### Theorem 158.1 — relative coloops and simultaneous release

For the monotone activation sequence \(A_0,\ldots,A_\ell\), let \(E_0\) be the old
active pairs, \(E_{\rm new}\) the \(\ell\) created pairs, and \(V_0\) the vertices
incident with \(E_0\). Then \(E_{\rm new}\) is a forest whose components meet \(V_0\) in
at most one vertex, and

\[
\operatorname{rank}(E_0\text{ rigidity rows}+E_{\rm new}\text{ rigidity rows})
=\operatorname{rank}(E_0\text{ rigidity rows})+\ell.
\]

Moreover, after fixing \(V_0\) (and one root in each rootless activation component),
the activation edge derivatives can be prescribed arbitrarily. In particular there
is a finite velocity whose derivative is \(+1\) on every created unit pair and \(-1\)
on every created diameter pair. For all sufficiently small positive amplitudes this
produces a triangle-free global minimizer of the same diameter, with exactly the old
active pairs \(E_0\); all vertices outside \(V_0\) are strict rattlers.

#### Proof

Before its move, \(r_i\) is strict and isolated in the active graph. The new pair
\(r_iq_i\) therefore attaches a previously isolated vertex. Persistence makes \(r_i\)
permanently non-strict, so no creator is repeated; if \(q_i\) is a creator it was
created earlier. Induction gives the forest and one-root assertions.

No creator is incident with an old active pair, since old pairs persist and would have
prevented it from being strict. In a row dependence among old and new rigidity rows,
peel a leaf outside \(V_0\): its coordinate block occurs in only its incident new row,
forcing that coefficient to vanish. Repetition proves the rank formula.

For an oriented activation tree, fix its root and, for arbitrary \(\lambda_e\), set

\[
v_w-v_u=\lambda_e\frac{p_w-p_u}{|p_w-p_u|^2}
\]

along each oriented edge \(u\\to w\). This realizes every prescribed derivative and
keeps all old active derivatives zero. Taking the signs \(+1\) for unit contacts and
\(-1\) for diameter pairs gives, for small \(t>0\),

\[
|p_x+tv_x-p_y-tv_y|^2=1+2t+t^2|v_x-v_y|^2>1
\]

on created unit edges, and strictly less than \(D^2\) on created diameter edges.
Inactive pairs retain their strict margins; an old diameter pair remains fixed, so the
diameter is exactly \(D=D(|A^*|)\). The contact graph is a subgraph of the terminal
triangle-free graph, and all active pairs incident with an outside vertex have been
released. This proves the theorem.

### Theorem 159.1 — boundary-transmitting locked-triangle certificate

Let \(H\) be a full-dimensional convex polygon and \(P\subset H\) a finite
unit-separated set. If a unit equilateral triangle is universally locked in the
fixed-container expansive cone, then there are nonnegative contact coefficients
\(\alpha_{ij}\) and active-facet coefficients \(\mu_{if}\), with all three triangle
edges having positive \(\alpha\), such that

\[
\sum_j\alpha_{ij}(p_i-p_j)-\sum_f\mu_{if}n_f=0
\]

at every vertex. The positive-contact component containing the triangle has
nonzero facet reaction at at least two distinct points of \(P\cap\partial H\).

#### Proof

Write the cone generators as the nonnegative contact derivatives \(a_g\) and the
active-facet derivatives \(b_{if}=-n_f\cdot v_i\). Universal locking gives both
\(a_e\) and \(-a_e\) nonnegative on the cone for every triangle edge. Farkas's
polar-cone theorem therefore gives

\[
-a_e=\sum_g\lambda_g^{(e)}a_g+\sum_{i,f}\nu_{if}^{(e)}b_{if},
\qquad \lambda_g^{(e)},\nu_{if}^{(e)}\ge0.
\]

Summing the three identities and moving the left side yields a zero functional with
contact coefficient at least one on every triangle edge. Reading vertex blocks gives
the displayed equilibrium. Summing it over the positive-contact component cancels
internal forces and leaves a zero sum of outward facet normals. If that component had
no facet reaction, a uniquely extreme vertex would contradict its nonzero positive
contact equilibrium. If all reactions were at one boundary point, the pointed normal
cone of a full-dimensional polygon would make a nonzero nonnegative zero sum
impossible. Hence two distinct boundary points carry reaction, and connectivity gives
contact paths from the triangle to both.

## 3. Exact target implication

Theorem 158.1 rules out a universal critical-cone blockage or signed/KKT load created
by activation rows alone; Theorem 159.1 upgrades a locked local triangle to a
boundary-transmitting source-hull stress. Neither changes the response quantity
\(\zeta\), because the source-hull boundary points are not canonically the response
terminals \(C^\partial\).

## 4. Exact remaining lemma

**Locked-support transport lemma.** For a first bad capacity transition built from the
terminal activated source, map the boundary reaction paths of Theorem 159.1, or the
finite directions of Theorem 158.1, into the directed alternating source-to-exterior
network. Either one certificate gives an independent saved source terminal and \(s\)
independent certificates give a rank-\(s\) subspace of \(\ker S\), or the associated
source replacement has diameter strictly below \(D\).

No such map is proved.

## 5. Audit classification and regressions

| Claim | Classification | Audit result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Activation rows are relative coloops | proved | Leaf elimination works modulo the old active rows. |
| All activations can be released at once | proved | Tree recursion realizes prescribed edge derivatives and strict margins persist. |
| Activation rank forces KKT load | rejected | Section 153 already gives universal zero load; the new signed result strengthens the no-go. |
| A locked triangle supplies a Schur separator | unproved | The certificate reaches \(P\cap\partial H\), not \(C^\partial\). |
| Local cage plus planarity forces response nullity | rejected | Section 149 supplies full-rank planar response data. |

The exact two-leaf cage survives because its whole-cage universal lock is not assumed;
the Section 152 non-global activation/monomer regression survives; the Section 153
zero-KKT theorem is strengthened; straight chains remain compatible with quadratic
opening; regular shells and diameter stars can carry the boundary stress; cyclic and
overlapping circuits and annular full-linkage responses remain outside the theorem.

## 6. Literature and computation

No external theorem or computation is used in Sections 158–159. The only invoked
ingredient beyond the canonical modules is finite-dimensional Farkas/polar-cone
duality and elementary Euclidean geometry.

